WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.565
Same file, same prompt, two engines.

00:00:02.945 --> 00:00:07.015
This week one new engine claimed over a thousand tokens a second

00:00:07.015 --> 00:00:08.711
on a six gigabyte laptop.

00:00:08.711 --> 00:00:13.460
Another app claimed a hundred and twenty-five billion parameters on a twelve gigabyte card.

00:00:15.957 --> 00:00:16.847
Hi, I'm Tachyon.

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:18.034
Both claims are real.

00:00:18.034 --> 00:00:21.000
Both lost their conditions on the way to the post.

00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:25.357
Here is the formula.

00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:28.416
Decoding reads the weights once a pass.

00:00:28.416 --> 00:00:30.287
Bandwidth over bytes is the ceiling.

00:00:30.707 --> 00:00:32.350
A real step reaches part of it.

00:00:34.337 --> 00:00:37.100
Speculation lets one pass commit several tokens.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:39.279
Batching lets several users share it.

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.180
The model is one point one gigabytes.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.728
Plain decoding reads it once for every token.

00:00:47.148 --> 00:00:50.505
An edit is mostly the prompt again, so a drafter copies guesses

00:00:50.505 --> 00:00:52.742
from it, and one pass checks them all.

00:00:53.162 --> 00:00:57.357
Run the same edit again, and Kinetic's draft index already holds the answer.

00:00:57.357 --> 00:00:59.292
Up to nine tokens a pass.

00:00:59.712 --> 00:01:02.330
That is how one stream gets past the ceiling.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:04.075
About five tokens for every read.

00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:09.191
The edit numbers on this page are for a repeated edit.

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:13.473
I built Kinetic on a three-oh-ninety.

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:16.999
The first edit runs at about twelve hundred tokens a second.

00:01:16.999 --> 00:01:18.922
Repeat it, and it nearly doubles.

00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:25.496
For new text, Kinetic and llama.cpp read the same bytes at the same speed.

00:01:25.496 --> 00:01:28.863
The win is for agents that resend the same files.

00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:33.982
Linger's figure says the two bit file is thirty-nine gigabytes.

00:01:34.362 --> 00:01:36.212
That is one of two shards.

00:01:36.212 --> 00:01:38.678
The second is a twenty-nine gigabyte n-gram table.

00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:43.675
A twelve gigabyte card holds about one expert in six.

00:01:44.075 --> 00:01:47.114
All of them wait in RAM, and the CPU computes the misses

00:01:47.114 --> 00:01:48.128
at the same time.

00:01:48.528 --> 00:01:52.342
The SSD holds the lookup table, read a few rows a token.

00:01:52.342 --> 00:01:54.884
So the claim needs sixty-four gigabytes of RAM.

00:01:56.217 --> 00:01:59.494
The forty-five comes from a community run with drafting on.

00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:03.755
Linger's own row for a twelve gigabyte card with less RAM is twenty-one.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.188
Kinetic's number is one stream, repeating an edit.

00:02:08.907 --> 00:02:12.831
Linger's needs the RAM, and its own installer sizes that model for

00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:14.139
a twenty-four gigabyte card.

00:02:15.808 --> 00:02:17.587
Both numbers survive the arithmetic.

00:02:17.587 --> 00:02:20.790
What they do not survive is losing their conditions.

00:02:23.525 --> 00:02:27.238
Bandwidth sets the ceiling, drafts beat it for one stream, and RAM

00:02:27.238 --> 00:02:28.476
carries the big model.

00:02:28.476 --> 00:02:29.404
Thanks for watching!

00:02:29.824 --> 00:02:32.012
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:32.012 --> 00:02:32.637
I'm Tachyon.

00:02:32.637 --> 00:02:32.949
Bye!
